diff --git a/docs/decisions/3567-ramp-entry-at-dead-end.md b/docs/decisions/3567-ramp-entry-at-dead-end.md
index b415e8d64..6dbb64ad4 100644
--- a/docs/decisions/3567-ramp-entry-at-dead-end.md
+++ b/docs/decisions/3567-ramp-entry-at-dead-end.md
@@ -1,51 +1,107 @@
 ## #3567 - A later Outside pass enters by a ramp along the pass at a dead end
 
-**Decided.** The maintainer set the entry in the question dialog on
-2026-11-22 (the comments on #3567). At a dead end of a later 2D Offset pass
-with "Pass starts at: Outside", the tool enters by a ramp along the pass.
-The ramp lowers the tool while it travels along the pass. The cut engages in
-a gradual way. The late-entry pin counts only plunges and helices into
-stock. A ramp along the pass is not one of them. Climb milling stays. The
-full-width rule stays. The "Auto" workaround stays until this fix merges.
-
-The worker made these choices unattended:
-
-1. **The dead end is read from the cleared column.** `pass_link` already
-   asks `plunge_law::into_solid` at a later pass start. A start that a lead
-   chord reached from cleared stock has a cleared column, so the test is
-   false. A dead end has no such lead, so the test is true. No new per-pass
-   flag is needed from `level_order`.
-2. **The ramp goes before the helix and the plunge.** At such a start,
-   `ramp_fallback` plans the existing zig-zag ramp along the pass at the
-   entry ramp angle (3 degrees by default). The helix and the plunge remain
-   the entry at every other start.
-3. **The scope is Outside with Waterline or Offset rings.** The flag is set
-   for `PassStart::Outside` and those two strategies, on the main pocket
-   path and on the "Do not touch" path. Auto, Inside, Adaptive, Zigzag and
-   rest machining keep their old entry law.
-4. **A ramp that does not fit keeps the old law.** The pass can be too
-   short for the ramp angle and the leg limit. Then the existing helix,
-   plunge or named refusal stands. No refusal is weakened.
-5. **The pin is narrowed, not removed.** `late_entry_cut` counts only a
-   descending `MoveKind::Plunge` or a descending arc (a helix). A straight
-   descending feed is the ramp and is not counted.
-
-**Why.** Main posts a helix or a plunge at each later dead-end start of the
-split band. The tool disc meets the crescent of uncut stock at the full tool
-width. That is a tool-load fault: the cutter can break. The pin read
-332.88 mm2 of stock cut by those later entries on main. With the ramp it
-reads 0.00 mm2.
+**The maintainer decision** (issue #3567, 2026-11-22, given in the
+question dialog):
+
+> At a dead end of a later pass of 2D Offset with "Pass starts at: Outside",
+> the tool enters with **a ramp along the pass**. Option 1 of the worker's
+> comment.
+>
+> - The ramp lowers the tool while it travels along the pass. The cut
+>   engages in a gradual way.
+> - The late-entry pin is narrowed to count only plunges and helices into
+>   stock. A ramp along the pass is not one of them.
+> - Climb milling stays. The full-width rule (no flat feed wider than one
+>   stepover) stays.
+> - The workaround "Pass starts at: Auto" stays until the fix merges.
+
+**The coordinator's decision by the owner's standing word** (issue #3567,
+2026-11-22T23:24Z):
+
+> Maintainer decision (owner's standing word 2026-11-22: choose recommended
+> while he sleeps). Options: (1) accept the full-width ramp at the ramp angle
+> and state it in the decision file, the PR and the changelog; (2) stop the
+> work until the maintainer confirms; (3) find an entry that never cuts at
+> the full width (it needs the reversed walk that the maintainer dropped).
+> Taken: **(1)**. Reason: it is what "a ramp along the pass" gives by
+> geometry, the angle limits the load, and the alternatives were dropped in
+> the decision. If the maintainer wants a lower load, the ramp angle of the
+> operation is the control.
+
+**What the ramp is, in plain words.** A dead end has the part wall behind
+the tool and uncut stock in front of it. The ramp is an entry INTO that
+uncut stock: the tool goes down along its own pass at the ramp angle
+(3 degrees when the operation sets none), out and back along the start of
+the pass (the existing zig-zag of the ramp fallback). Where the ring cut
+before does not reach the dead end, the ramp cuts a slot at nearly the full
+tool width, at the ramp angle. After the ramp, the pass cuts at one
+stepover. The ramp angle is the control of the load: a smaller angle gives
+a longer, lighter ramp. The late-entry pin reads 0.00 mm2 because it does
+not count ramp moves, not because the ramp cuts no stock.
+
+Readings on the band of the pins (stock 100 x 40, part R 15, tool D8,
+stepover 4, depth 3, no ramp angle set):
+
+- 8 dead ends. Each ramp runs at 3.000 degrees from Z 2.0 (the top plus
+  the 2 mm plunge height) to Z -3.0, 95.41 mm along the pass (one out and
+  back leg of 47.7 mm). The pass then cuts that stretch again at the floor.
+- Where each ramp starts, about 25 percent of the tool disc (about
+  12.6 mm2 of 50.3 mm2) is uncut stock on the 2-D grid of the pins. While
+  the ramp travels, the width of uncut stock on the leading half of the rim
+  reads 4.780 mm at most (the stepover band). The coordinator's comment
+  gives about 83 percent (41.6 mm2 of 50.3 mm2): 41.6 mm2 is 332.88 mm2 / 8,
+  the stock that each helix of main cut over its whole swept orbit, not the
+  stock in the disc at the start. On this band the ring before reaches the
+  dead end; the full-width slot is the case where it does not.
+- The late-entry pin: 332.88 mm2 on main, 0.00 mm2 with the ramp.
+
+**What was decided by the workers, inside those decisions.**
+
+1. **The plan marks the dead ends.** `level_order::order` marks each later
+   pass of an Outside plan that no lead-in reaches (`LevelPlan::ramp_starts`).
+   The ladder enters only a marked start by the ramp
+   (`level_ramp_entry::dead_end_ramp`, called first in
+   `ladder_links::pass_link`). The first entry of each level and the ring 0
+   pieces keep the entry of `main`: a helix or a plunge into solid stock
+   there is the normal first entry and is outside this issue. The Auto-order
+   plan after a completeness-guard fallback has no marks, so it stays pure
+   Auto (pinned byte for byte). The first head of the PR read the dead end
+   from `plunge_law::into_solid` at each pass link; that changed ring 0
+   pieces and the guard-fallback plan too, and scanned all earlier moves at
+   each pass link.
+2. **The ramp is the existing ramp fallback.** `descent::ramp_fallback`
+   plans the zig-zag along the first cut at the entry's ramp angle, 3 degrees
+   (`links::DEFAULT_RAMP_ANGLE`) when the operation sets none, with the
+   entry's maximum ramp length as the leg limit. No second ramp law.
+3. **No silent plunge.** The plan checks each dead end before it is kept
+   (`level_ramp_entry::too_short_at`, at the deepest descent of any cut
+   level). A dead end too short for the ramp sends the area to the Auto
+   order with `StartSide::ramp_fallback_note`, which carries
+   `RINGS_FALLBACK`, so the op raises `Warning::StrategyFallback`. If the
+   ladder still cannot plan the ramp, it names that on the path.
+4. **A replaced entry is named.** When the ramp stands in for the user's
+   Plunge, Helix or Arc entry, the path says so once, in the #2265 stand-in
+   form (`level_ramp_entry::stand_in_note`).
+5. **The scope.** 2D Offset and 2D Pocket with "Pass starts at: Outside" and
+   the Waterline or Offset rings strategy (the main pocket path and the
+   "Do not touch" path). Auto, Inside, Adaptive, Zigzag and rest machining
+   mark no dead end.
+6. **The pin is narrowed, not removed.** `late_entry_cut` counts each entry
+   move that is not a ramp along the pass. A ramp is a feed no steeper than
+   the ramp angle plus 0.05 degrees whose centre stays within 0.02 mm of a
+   floor feed all along. A plunge, a helix and a steep straight feed count.
+   The pin also asserts that a ramp ran.
 
 **Dropped.**
 - A planar lead chord across one stepover (the held option A on
   `issue/3567-outside-later-entry`, commit `12c0fd125`): it feeds into the
   crescent at the full tool width and breaks the shape-mask law.
-- The named fallback to the Auto order: it changes the band order and
-  breaks the same shape-mask law.
-- A per-pass flag from `level_order`: `into_solid` already names the dead
-  end, so the flag adds plumbing with no gain.
-
-**Cost to change later.** Low. The rule is one branch in
-`machining2d/ladder_links.rs::pass_link` and one `Motion` flag set by the
-callers. To make the ramp the entry for Inside too, set the flag for Inside
-and run the Inside pins.
+- The Auto order at each dead end: the Auto order slots the band.
+- A reversed walk at a dead end: it breaks climb milling.
+- The dead end read from `into_solid` at every pass link (the first head of
+  the PR): see item 1.
+
+**Cost to change later.** Low. The marks are one list on the plan and on
+`Motion`; the entry is one function in `machining2d/level_ramp_entry.rs`.
+To make the ramp the entry for Inside too, mark Inside dead ends in
+`level_order::order` and run the Inside pins.

From d41c2b76abcfc2d66ded1d98668fe71d8d07b80e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NeoTech <andreas@roughedge.se>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 01:15:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] perf(2d): thin the dead-end ramp's lead within the arc
 fit budget

A level curve of a meshed part has a vertex every few microns, and the
zig-zag ramp was posted on each one: about 23 500 ramp moves on a dense
band in place of about 760. The ramp now rides the start of the pass
(as far as the ramp can use) thinned by Douglas-Peucker within
ARC_FIT_TOL; each kept point is a vertex of the pass. Pinned by
`the_ramp_lead_is_thinned_within_the_fit_budget`.

Refs #3567

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../src/machining2d/level_ramp_entry.rs       | 80 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 76 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/crates/motion/src/machining2d/level_ramp_entry.rs b/crates/motion/src/machining2d/level_ramp_entry.rs
index a0e98325a..4dea304fe 100644
--- a/crates/motion/src/machining2d/level_ramp_entry.rs
+++ b/crates/motion/src/machining2d/level_ramp_entry.rs
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@ use crate::entry::Entry3D;
 use crate::geo2d::Point2;
 use crate::links::plan_zig_capped;
 
+use super::arcs::ARC_FIT_TOL;
 use super::descent::{Cuts, Lvl, fallback_ramp_angle, push, ramp_fallback};
 use super::equal_steps::cut_levels_for;
 use super::ladder_links::LevelWalk;
@@ -102,8 +103,16 @@ pub(super) fn dead_end_ramp(b: &mut PathBuilder, lv: &LevelWalk, m: &Motion, cer
         return None;
     }
     let cen = cert?;
-    let l = Lvl { cen, passes: lv.passes, pass: lv.pass, walk: lv.walk, mat_top: lv.mat_top };
-    let from = (lv.mat_top + m.links.approach.plunge_height).min(plane);
+    let a = &m.links.approach;
+    let from = (lv.mat_top + a.plunge_height).min(plane);
+    // The ramp rides the start of the pass thinned within the arc fit's
+    // budget: a level curve of a meshed part has a vertex every few
+    // microns, and a ramp posted on each one is tens of thousands of moves.
+    let tan = fallback_ramp_angle(a).to_radians().tan();
+    let drop = (m.top_z + a.plunge_height).max(from) - lv.z;
+    let need = if tan.is_finite() && tan > 0.0 { (0.5 * drop / tan).max(a.ramp_max_len) } else { f64::INFINITY };
+    let lead = thinned(lead_of(lv.pass, need), ARC_FIT_TOL);
+    let l = Lvl { cen, passes: lv.passes, pass: &lead, walk: lv.walk, mat_top: lv.mat_top };
     let Some(d) = ramp_fallback(&l, lv.z, from, m) else {
         b.note_once(&format!(
             "entry: the dead-end pass at ({:.3}, {:.3}) is too short for a ramp along it - it takes the pass link's entry",
@@ -117,9 +126,54 @@ pub(super) fn dead_end_ramp(b: &mut PathBuilder, lv: &LevelWalk, m: &Motion, cer
     Some(push(b, &d, &l, lv.z, plane, m))
 }
 
+/// The start of `pts` up to the first vertex at or past `len` mm of
+/// travel (all of it when shorter): the stretch a zig-zag ramp can use.
+fn lead_of(pts: &[Point2], len: f64) -> &[Point2] {
+    let mut run = 0.0;
+    for (i, w) in pts.windows(2).enumerate() {
+        run += (w[1].x - w[0].x).hypot(w[1].y - w[0].y);
+        if run >= len {
+            return &pts[..i + 2];
+        }
+    }
+    pts
+}
+
+/// `pts` thinned by Douglas-Peucker to the vertices the polyline needs
+/// within `tol` mm: each kept point is a vertex of `pts`, each dropped one
+/// lies within `tol` of the chord that replaces it. Iterative, so a long
+/// walk cannot overflow the stack.
+fn thinned(pts: &[Point2], tol: f64) -> Vec<Point2> {
+    let n = pts.len();
+    if n < 3 {
+        return pts.to_vec();
+    }
+    let mut keep = vec![false; n];
+    keep[0] = true;
+    keep[n - 1] = true;
+    let mut todo = vec![(0usize, n - 1)];
+    while let Some((i, j)) = todo.pop() {
+        let (a, b) = (pts[i], pts[j]);
+        let far = (i + 1..j).map(|k| (seg_dist(pts[k], a, b), k)).fold((0.0, i), |m, d| if d.0 > m.0 { d } else { m });
+        if far.0 > tol && far.1 > i {
+            keep[far.1] = true;
+            todo.push((i, far.1));
+            todo.push((far.1, j));
+        }
+    }
+    pts.iter().zip(keep).filter_map(|(p, k)| k.then_some(*p)).collect()
+}
+
+fn seg_dist(p: Point2, a: Point2, b: Point2) -> f64 {
+    let (dx, dy) = (b.x - a.x, b.y - a.y);
+    let dd = dx * dx + dy * dy;
+    let t = if dd > 0.0 && dd.is_finite() { (((p.x - a.x) * dx + (p.y - a.y) * dy) / dd).clamp(0.0, 1.0) } else { 0.0 };
+    (p.x - a.x - t * dx).hypot(p.y - a.y - t * dy)
+}
+
